digraph circuit_design {
    graph [
        goal="Design an electronic circuit that blinks a red LED at 1 Hz from an automotive 12V source, and produce a validated ngspice netlist.",
        rankdir=TB,
        default_max_retry="2",
        loop_restart_signature_limit="5",
        model_stylesheet="
            * { llm_model: claude-sonnet-4.6; llm_provider: anthropic; }
        "
    ]

    start [shape=Mdiamond, label="Start"]
    exit  [shape=Msquare, label="Exit"]

    define_requirements [
        shape=box,
        auto_status=true,
        prompt="Goal: $goal

Create a concise engineering requirements document for this circuit request.

Reference docs are available in docs/electronics/. Read docs/electronics/00-index.md first — it lists
all available topics with one-line descriptions. Read whichever docs are relevant to this task before
proceeding. At minimum, review the design procedures and common mistakes docs.

Write the result to:
- .ai/runs/$KILROY_RUN_ID/circuit_requirements.md

The requirements document must include:
- the functional behavior implied by the goal, with quantitative acceptance criteria where possible
- operating assumptions for the power source and environment implied by the goal
- output/load assumptions, including current, voltage, timing, and tolerance constraints as applicable
- component constraints, including preference for widely available parts and simple topology
- a verification plan that can be checked later without ambiguity

Keep the document under 250 lines. Be concrete and quantitative.

Write to $KILROY_STAGE_STATUS_PATH (fallback: $KILROY_STAGE_STATUS_FALLBACK_PATH) ONLY on failure:
{\"status\":\"fail\",\"failure_reason\":\"...\",\"failure_class\":\"deterministic\"}"
    ]

    choose_topology [
        shape=box,
        auto_status=true,
        prompt="Goal: $goal

Read:
- .ai/runs/$KILROY_RUN_ID/circuit_requirements.md

Reference docs are available in docs/electronics/. Read docs/electronics/00-index.md to discover
relevant topics, then read the docs that apply to the component types and circuit techniques
you are considering (e.g., oscillators, transistors, diodes, power regulation).

Choose a practical circuit topology for this request and justify it. Prefer a robust, easy-to-build design over novelty.

IMPORTANT — component simulability constraint:
The circuit will be validated with ngspice simulation. The netlist generation tool supports these
component types natively: R, C, L, D (diodes/LEDs), Q (BJTs: 2N3904/2N3906), and voltage/current sources.
For ICs (e.g., NE555, op-amps), the tool uses external SPICE model files from models/kicad-spice-library/.
Before choosing an IC, check that a compatible .lib/.sub file exists in that directory.
If no model is available for a desired IC, prefer a discrete transistor-based design that can be simulated
reliably with standard SPICE primitives.

Write these artifacts:
- .ai/runs/$KILROY_RUN_ID/topology_decision.md
- .ai/runs/$KILROY_RUN_ID/preliminary_bom.md

The topology decision must cover:
- the core circuit approach and why it is suitable for the goal
- whether regulation, filtering, reverse-polarity protection, and transient suppression are needed
- every major component and its role
- the key sizing logic for component values, including timing or load-drive calculations when relevant
- any design risks or assumptions that should be reviewed later
- which SPICE model files will be needed (list the .lib/.sub paths from models/kicad-spice-library/)

The BOM must list one component per line with reference designator class, suggested part, and purpose.

Write to $KILROY_STAGE_STATUS_PATH (fallback: $KILROY_STAGE_STATUS_FALLBACK_PATH) ONLY on failure:
{\"status\":\"fail\",\"failure_reason\":\"...\",\"failure_class\":\"deterministic\"}"
    ]

    produce_design [
        shape=box,
        auto_status=true,
        prompt="Goal: $goal

Read:
- .ai/runs/$KILROY_RUN_ID/circuit_requirements.md
- .ai/runs/$KILROY_RUN_ID/topology_decision.md
- .ai/runs/$KILROY_RUN_ID/preliminary_bom.md

Reference docs are available in docs/electronics/. Read docs/electronics/00-index.md to discover
relevant topics. At minimum, review the common mistakes doc before finalizing your design.

If .ai/runs/$KILROY_RUN_ID/failure_analysis.md exists, this is a design revision after
simulation failure. Read it FIRST and:
- Address every issue identified in the failure analysis
- Do NOT repeat the same design choices that caused the failure
- Explain what changed and why in the circuit_design.md

Produce the actual circuit design artifacts for this request.

Write:
- .ai/runs/$KILROY_RUN_ID/circuit_design.md
- .ai/runs/$KILROY_RUN_ID/final_bom.csv
- .ai/runs/$KILROY_RUN_ID/schematic_notes.md

Requirements for circuit_design.md:
- describe the full circuit in clear implementation order
- name all nodes and key component values
- include the calculations needed to justify the design, such as timing, biasing, gain, filtering, or current-limit calculations as applicable
- describe input protection, decoupling, and environmental robustness choices appropriate to the goal
- include assembly notes and expected behavior at startup

Requirements for final_bom.csv:
- header row: item,designator,part,value_or_package,purpose
- one component per row

Requirements for schematic_notes.md:
- textual net-by-net connection notes sufficient for a human to draw the schematic
- include polarity-sensitive parts and orientation notes

Do not leave placeholders. If a value is estimated, say so explicitly and keep it realistic.

Write to $KILROY_STAGE_STATUS_PATH (fallback: $KILROY_STAGE_STATUS_FALLBACK_PATH) ONLY on failure:
{\"status\":\"fail\",\"failure_reason\":\"...\",\"failure_class\":\"deterministic\"}"
    ]

    generate_circuit_json [
        shape=box,
        auto_status=true,
        prompt="Goal: $goal

Read:
- .ai/runs/$KILROY_RUN_ID/circuit_requirements.md
- .ai/runs/$KILROY_RUN_ID/circuit_design.md
- .ai/runs/$KILROY_RUN_ID/final_bom.csv
- .ai/runs/$KILROY_RUN_ID/schematic_notes.md

Convert the circuit design into a structured JSON file that a deterministic tool will
use to generate an ngspice netlist. Write the JSON to:
- .ai/runs/$KILROY_RUN_ID/circuit.json

The JSON schema is:
{
  \"title\": \"<descriptive title>\",
  \"includes\": [
    // Paths to SPICE model files relative to models/kicad-spice-library/
    // Example: \"Models/Manufacturer/TRT-Electronics/2n3904.lib\"
    // Example: \"Models/Diode/led.lib\"
    // List the model library files identified in topology_decision.md
  ],
  \"components\": [
    // Two-terminal passives (R, C, L):
    {\"ref\": \"R1\", \"type\": \"R\", \"value\": \"10k\", \"nodes\": [\"node_a\", \"node_b\"]},
    {\"ref\": \"C1\", \"type\": \"C\", \"value\": \"10u\", \"nodes\": [\"node_a\", \"0\"]},

    // DC voltage source:
    {\"ref\": \"V1\", \"type\": \"vdc\", \"value\": \"12\", \"nodes\": [\"pos_node\", \"neg_node\"]},

    // Diode or LED (model name must match the .model name inside the included .lib file):
    {\"ref\": \"D1\", \"type\": \"D\", \"model\": \"LED_GENERAL\", \"nodes\": [\"anode\", \"cathode\"]},

    // BJT transistor (model name must match the .model name inside the included .lib file):
    // nodes are [collector, base, emitter]
    {\"ref\": \"Q1\", \"type\": \"Q\", \"model\": \"Q2N3904\", \"nodes\": [\"collector\", \"base\", \"emitter\"]},

    // IC subcircuit (model name must match .subckt name inside the included .lib/.sub file):
    // pins are named, pin_order specifies the subcircuit pin order from the .subckt definition
    {\"ref\": \"U1\", \"type\": \"NE555\", \"subcircuit\": \"NE555\",
     \"pins\": {\"GND\": \"0\", \"TRIG\": \"node\", \"OUT\": \"node\", \"RESET\": \"node\",
               \"CTRL\": \"node\", \"THRESH\": \"node\", \"DISCH\": \"node\", \"VCC\": \"node\"},
     \"pin_order\": [\"GND\", \"TRIG\", \"OUT\", \"RESET\", \"CTRL\", \"THRESH\", \"DISCH\", \"VCC\"]}
  ],
  \"analysis\": {
    \"type\": \"tran\",
    \"step\": \"1ms\",
    \"stop\": \"5s\"
  },
  \"plots\": [
    {\"signals\": [\"v(out)\", \"v(thresh)\"], \"title\": \"description\"}
  ],
  \"measures\": [
    // Each measure defines a quantitative acceptance test on the simulation.
    // Supported types: max, min, avg, rms, peak_to_peak, frequency, period,
    //                  duty_cycle, find_when
    //
    // All measures require: id (lowercase_snake_case), description, type, signal, accept ({min, max})
    // Time-windowed types (max/min/avg/rms/peak_to_peak) also need: from, to
    // Crossing types (frequency/period/duty_cycle) also need: threshold, edge (\"rising\"/\"falling\"), from, to
    // find_when needs: value, edge, and optionally td (time delay before searching)

    {\"id\": \"led_blink_freq\", \"description\": \"LED blinks at ~1 Hz\",
     \"type\": \"frequency\", \"signal\": \"v(out)\", \"threshold\": 2.5,
     \"edge\": \"rising\", \"from\": \"1s\", \"to\": \"5s\",
     \"accept\": {\"min\": 0.8, \"max\": 1.2}},

    {\"id\": \"output_high\", \"description\": \"Output goes high when LED on\",
     \"type\": \"max\", \"signal\": \"v(out)\", \"from\": \"1s\", \"to\": \"5s\",
     \"accept\": {\"min\": 8.0, \"max\": 12.0}}
  ]
}

CRITICAL RULES:
- Node \"0\" is always ground.
- Every ref must start with the correct SPICE prefix letter (R, C, L, D, V for sources, Q for BJTs).
  For IC subcircuits, any ref is fine (the tool adds the X prefix automatically).
- Use SI suffix strings for values: \"10k\", \"47u\", \"100n\", \"330\", \"12\".
- All node names must be lowercase alphanumeric with underscores (no spaces or special chars).
- The analysis step should be small enough to capture the circuit behavior (1ms is fine for 1Hz blink).
- The stop time should be long enough to see several cycles.
- Include at least one plot with the key signals to observe.
- Write ONLY valid JSON — no comments, no trailing commas.
- Include at least 3 measures that verify the key functional requirements from circuit_requirements.md.
- The measures \"from\" time should skip initial transients (at least 1 second for circuits with startup behavior).
- The measures \"to\" time must not exceed the analysis stop time.
- Acceptance ranges should be realistic engineering tolerances (typically +/-20% for timing, +/-10% for voltages).
- Every measure must have a unique \"id\" using lowercase_snake_case.
- The measure \"signal\" must reference actual node names from the circuit (e.g., \"v(out)\", \"i(D1)\").
- Before writing JSON, verify that every component node name appears in at least one other component's node list (no floating nodes except ground \"0\").
- The \"includes\" array must list every external model file needed. Model names used in components (e.g., \"Q2N3904\", \"LED_GENERAL\") must match the .model or .subckt name defined inside the included file.

Write to $KILROY_STAGE_STATUS_PATH (fallback: $KILROY_STAGE_STATUS_FALLBACK_PATH) ONLY on failure:
{\"status\":\"fail\",\"failure_reason\":\"...\",\"failure_class\":\"deterministic\"}"
    ]

    convert_to_netlist [
        shape=parallelogram,
        label="Convert JSON to Netlist",
        tool_command="python3 tools/json2netlist.py .ai/runs/$KILROY_RUN_ID/circuit.json .ai/runs/$KILROY_RUN_ID/circuit.cir"
    ]

    run_simulation [
        shape=parallelogram,
        label="Run SPICE Simulation Tests",
        tool_command="python3 tools/check_measures.py .ai/runs/$KILROY_RUN_ID/circuit.cir .ai/runs/$KILROY_RUN_ID/circuit.measures_meta.json"
    ]

    check_simulation [shape=diamond, label="Simulation OK?"]

    analyze_failure [
        shape=box,
        auto_status=true,
        prompt="Goal: $goal

The SPICE simulation failed. Your job is to classify the failure and decide the best repair path.

Read:
- .ai/runs/$KILROY_RUN_ID/circuit_requirements.md
- .ai/runs/$KILROY_RUN_ID/topology_decision.md
- .ai/runs/$KILROY_RUN_ID/circuit_design.md
- .ai/runs/$KILROY_RUN_ID/circuit.json
- .ai/runs/$KILROY_RUN_ID/measures_results.json (if it exists)

Also read the failure dossier if available:
- .ai/runs/$KILROY_RUN_ID/failure_dossier.json

Classify the failure into one of two categories:

PARAMETRIC FIX — choose this when:
- Simulation ran and produced waveforms, but measure values are outside acceptance ranges
- The circuit topology is correct but component values need adjustment (RC timing, resistor sizing, etc.)
- Failed measures are within 2-3x of the acceptance range

DESIGN RETHINK — choose this when:
- ngspice convergence failure (singular matrix, no convergence, timestep too small)
- All measures ERROR or the circuit produces no meaningful output
- The output waveform shape is fundamentally wrong (e.g., no oscillation when expected, stuck at rail)
- A previous parametric fix attempt already failed (check if failure_analysis.md already exists)
- The failure is caused by a missing or incompatible SPICE model file

Write your analysis to:
- .ai/runs/$KILROY_RUN_ID/failure_analysis.md

The analysis must include:
- Which measures failed and by how much
- Root cause diagnosis
- Recommended fix approach
- Whether this is a parametric issue or a fundamental design issue

Then write your status with preferred_label set to your classification:
Write to $KILROY_STAGE_STATUS_PATH (fallback: $KILROY_STAGE_STATUS_FALLBACK_PATH):
{\"status\":\"success\",\"preferred_label\":\"parametric_fix\"} or
{\"status\":\"success\",\"preferred_label\":\"design_rethink\"}"
    ]

    review_failures [
        shape=box,
        auto_status=true,
        prompt="Goal: $goal

The SPICE simulation tests failed and the failure was classified as a parametric issue.

Read:
- .ai/runs/$KILROY_RUN_ID/circuit_requirements.md
- .ai/runs/$KILROY_RUN_ID/topology_decision.md
- .ai/runs/$KILROY_RUN_ID/circuit_design.md
- .ai/runs/$KILROY_RUN_ID/schematic_notes.md
- .ai/runs/$KILROY_RUN_ID/circuit.json
- .ai/runs/$KILROY_RUN_ID/measures_results.json
- .ai/runs/$KILROY_RUN_ID/failure_analysis.md

Analyze which measures failed and why. Common causes:
- Wrong component values (resistor too large/small for desired current)
- Timing component miscalculation (RC time constants)
- Incorrect node connections
- Missing or wrong analysis time window

Fix the circuit.json file to address the failures. You MUST:
1. Identify the root cause for each failed measure
2. Calculate the correct component values
3. Update ONLY the circuit.json file at .ai/runs/$KILROY_RUN_ID/circuit.json
4. Keep the measures array — do not remove failing tests
5. Explain your changes

Write to $KILROY_STAGE_STATUS_PATH (fallback: $KILROY_STAGE_STATUS_FALLBACK_PATH) ONLY on failure:
{\"status\":\"fail\",\"failure_reason\":\"...\",\"failure_class\":\"deterministic\"}"
    ]

    start -> define_requirements
    define_requirements -> choose_topology
    choose_topology -> produce_design
    produce_design -> generate_circuit_json
    generate_circuit_json -> convert_to_netlist
    convert_to_netlist -> run_simulation
    run_simulation -> check_simulation
    check_simulation -> exit [condition="outcome=success"]
    check_simulation -> analyze_failure [condition="outcome=fail && context.failure_class=deterministic"]
    check_simulation -> analyze_failure [condition="outcome=fail && context.failure_class=transient_infra"]
    check_simulation -> analyze_failure
    analyze_failure -> review_failures [label="parametric_fix"]
    analyze_failure -> produce_design [label="design_rethink"]
    analyze_failure -> review_failures
    review_failures -> convert_to_netlist
}
